    "q-161193": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161193",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{3}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
